  "id": "Compound::DB00726 - Gene::3269",
  "verbalization": "Trimipramine, Amoxapine, and Desipramine are all tricyclic antidepressants that share a common ancestor in the class of organic heterotricyclic compounds. The evidence suggests that Trimipramine causes Adenitis or Taste Peculiar, both of which are mediated by Amoxapine, which in turn binds to Hrh1. This indicates a potential mechanism where Trimipramine, through its interaction with Amoxapine, may influence Hrh1 via a shared biological pathway. The lowest common ancestor of Trimipramine and Amoxapine is the tricyclic antidepressant class, and the shared organic heterotricyclic compound ancestor further supports a common pharmacological profile. The evidence suggests that Trimipramine may interact with Hrh1 through a pathway involving Amoxapine, though the exact mechanism of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
